# 3. VISUAL DIRECTION — MAKE ADVIBE STUDIOS LOOK EXCEPTIONAL

## 3.1 Follow our actual brand identity

The existing Advibe Studios brand is the foundation of every design decision.

Study the current logo, colours, fonts, visual assets, and existing brand materials before deciding on the final aesthetic.

Do not arbitrarily replace our identity with a fashionable design trend.

Build a cohesive visual system that expresses a creative-led, professional, technically capable, modern agency.

The website should feel:

- Creative without being chaotic.
- Premium without being pretentious.
- Modern without feeling like a generic AI product.
- Energetic without being visually exhausting.
- Professional without looking corporate and lifeless.
- Beautiful without sacrificing clarity.
- Distinctive without sacrificing usability.

Avoid the standard AI landing-page formula of a generic gradient background, oversized headline, three identical feature cards, and repeated rounded rectangles.

Create a recognisable visual language that could belong specifically to Advibe Studios.

## 3.2 Typography is a major design priority

Typography must be one of the strongest parts of the visual experience.

Choose beautiful, highly legible typefaces that work together intentionally. Use the existing brand fonts if they are appropriate. Otherwise, select a complementary display typeface and a highly readable body typeface.

Establish a complete typographic system for:

- Hero headlines.
- Section headlines.
- Subheadings.
- Body copy.
- Captions.
- Statistics.
- Buttons.
- Navigation.
- Pricing.
- Course details.
- Case studies.
- Founder biographies.
- Frequently asked questions.

Large headings should have personality and presence. Body copy should be effortless to read, especially on mobile.

Use intentional font sizes, line heights, letter spacing, text widths, and font weights.

Avoid overly thin text, tiny labels, long lines, cramped paragraphs, and excessively wide headings.

Do not make the design beautiful by making the content difficult to read.

Use responsive typography that adjusts naturally to different screens without creating awkward line breaks or excessively large headings.

Load fonts efficiently. Prefer properly licensed, performant font files, and avoid downloading unnecessary weights or families.

## 3.3 Make backgrounds visually rich

Do not rely exclusively on flat, plain colour fills.

Use subtle, sophisticated surface treatments to create depth and visual interest while respecting the existing brand palette.

Depending on what suits the brand, explore:

- Very subtle grain or paper-like texture.
- Soft tonal transitions within the same brand colour.
- Restrained radial lighting.
- Layered background shapes.
- Fine-line patterns.
- Gentle vignettes.
- Carefully composed light and shadow.
- Low-contrast decorative geometry.
- Editorial-style image and typography compositions.
- Subtle textured surfaces behind important content.

These effects must be restrained and intentional. They should make the page feel richer, not noisier.

Avoid excessive gradients, high-contrast decorative patterns behind paragraphs, heavy glassmorphism, glowing neon borders, and unnecessary visual effects.

Textures must not interfere with text contrast or increase loading time unnecessarily. Prefer lightweight CSS or optimised assets over large decorative images.

## 3.4 Create a distinctive visual composition

Do not force every section into the same layout.

Use varied but coherent compositions, including appropriate combinations of:

- Large editorial typography.
- Asymmetric layouts.
- Strong image-led sections.
- Full-width visual moments.
- Carefully arranged video examples.
- Contrasting light and dark surfaces.
- Generous whitespace.
- Clear visual transitions.
- Beautifully designed comparison sections.
- Distinctive founder profiles.
- An immersive, readable case study presentation.

Every section should have a clear purpose and visual identity, while still belonging to the same design system.

Avoid making every section a centred heading followed by a row of cards.

Use real creative assets from the existing project wherever possible. The work we showcase should reinforce our credibility as a creative agency.

