# 4. MOBILE-LED DESIGN — NOT DESKTOP DESIGN SHRUNK DOWN

Mobile is the primary design environment for this project.

Do not simply build a desktop page and make it responsive afterward.

Design the experience from the perspective of someone discovering Advibe Studios through Instagram, TikTok, WhatsApp, or another mobile channel.

The mobile experience should be so good that it feels like the natural home of the website, while the desktop version expands into a more spacious, editorial experience.

## Mobile design requirements

- Design the first viewport deliberately.
- Make the main headline immediately understandable.
- Keep the primary call to action easy to find.
- Use readable font sizes and comfortable line heights.
- Use generous spacing between paragraphs and interactive elements.
- Avoid cramped multi-column layouts.
- Ensure buttons are easy to tap.
- Make images and videos fit naturally within the screen.
- Prevent horizontal overflow.
- Avoid long, uninterrupted walls of text.
- Use accordions, progressive disclosure, and clear section breaks where they improve comprehension.
- Make pricing, bonuses, guarantees, and course details easy to understand.
- Ensure important information is not hidden behind hover-only interactions.
- Keep sticky actions subtle and useful rather than intrusive.
- Ensure animations remain smooth on ordinary mobile devices.
- Test touch interactions rather than assuming desktop interactions will translate.

Use a mobile-first information hierarchy. The order in which information appears on mobile should reflect the way a prospective student makes a buying decision.

Do not simply stack desktop cards vertically and call the result mobile-first.

On desktop, take advantage of additional space through more expressive typography, layered imagery, wider compositions, and well-proportioned content areas.

The result must feel intentionally designed at both sizes.

